### Update tracking details (FBM only)
To allow shipment tracking, you need to provide the `courier` and `tracking_code` for each order shipment.

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> This endpoint is available only for FBM (Fulfilled By Merchant) orders
>
> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
>The existing API endpoint is limited to updating tracking details only for orders shipped to customers and does not currently support return orders.
>These should be still handled through merchants panel for the time being.

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/ecommerce/orders/:code/tracking_details
</pre>


<%= render_recording :merchants_ecommerce_orders_tracking_details %>

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://api.skroutz.gr/merchants/ecommerce/orders/191128-1234567/tracking_details \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.skroutz+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{ "tracking_details": [ { "courier":"acs", "tracking_code":"224085551" } ] }'
</pre>


### Request the voucher for a preorder (early dispatch)

When a preorder order has been accepted and you receive the product earlier than the
scheduled shipment creation date, you can request the courier voucher manually. This
is the same action available through the **"Create voucher now"** button in the merchants'
panel: it creates the voucher for the shipment.

The pickup location and pickup window are derived server-side, so no request body is
required.

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> This endpoint is available only for **preorder** orders.

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> A successful response only means the request was accepted. The voucher is generated
> asynchronously by the courier and is **not** returned in the response body. Once it is
> ready, it is delivered through the [order webhook](/smart_cart/orders_webhook) (as an
> `order_updated` event carrying the `courier_voucher`), and it is also available on the
> [order](#retrieve-a-single-order) via its `courier_voucher` attribute.

**Prerequisites:**

* Order must be in `accepted` state
* Order must be a preorder
* Order must not already have a voucher (shipment)
* A pickup location must be configured for the shop
* A pickup window must be currently available

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/ecommerce/orders/:code/request_voucher
</pre>

No request body required.

#### Example
<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/orders/191128-1234567/request_voucher \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here'
</pre>

#### Success Response

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "success": true
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Error Responses

**Order not found:**

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "order_error",
      "messages": [
        "Order not found"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

**Order not eligible** (not an accepted preorder, or a voucher already exists):

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "order_error",
      "messages": [
        "Cannot request voucher: order is not an accepted preorder without a voucher."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>


### File a compensation request

For an order that came back from the customer or the courier, you can ask Skroutz to compensate you for
line items that were returned damaged, incomplete or wrong, or were never returned. This is the same
action as the **compensation request** form in the merchants' panel, and it follows exactly the same
rules. A successful call files the request; Skroutz then reviews it, as it does requests filed from the
panel. You can follow it by [retrieving the order](#compensation-requests-on-the-order) with
`compensation_requests=1`, or in the merchants' panel.

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/ecommerce/orders/:code/compensation_requests
</pre>

**When an order can be compensated:**

* The order was returned (fully or partially), returned to sender, replaced, or cancelled after its
  shipment had been picked up.
* The request is filed within the compensation window. It is 7 working days from the delivery of the
  return to you (30 days for FBS orders, 50 days for shops with an extended window), or 15 working days
  from payment when the order has no return date. A return that is late becomes claimable as a
  `delayed_return` 35 days after its pickup (45 days for cargo), for 7 working days.
* Fulfilled by Merchant (FBM) orders generally cannot be compensated; the `not_eligible` message says so
  when this applies.
* The order has not already been fully compensated, and has no service request under inspection.
* No condition review of the returned products is pending. Submit it first, from the merchants' panel or
  with the [Returns API](/smart_cart/returns_api#submit-the-physical-review-of-a-return).
* The shop is under its compensation cap, as shown in the merchants' panel.

#### Request body

Send the request as `multipart/form-data`, with one `line_items` entry per line item you want
compensated. A request without files may also be sent as JSON. The entries may be sent as an array
(`line_items[][id]`) or keyed by index (`line_items[0][id]`), as HTML forms send them.

Name                                       | Type    | Description
------------------------------------------ | ------- | -----------------------------------------------------------
`line_items[_].id`                         | String  | The line item's `id`, as the [order](#retrieve-a-single-order) gives it. Required.
`line_items[_].quantity`                   | Integer | From 1 up to the quantity still eligible for compensation. Required.
`line_items[_].reason`                     | String  | One of the reasons below that the line item takes. Required.
`line_items[_].at_fault`                   | String  | Who is at fault: `customer` or `courier`. Required by most reasons, see below.
`line_items[_].comment`                    | String  | A description of the damage, up to 400 characters. Required by most reasons, see below.
`line_items[_].product_images[]`           | File    | Photos of the product: PNG or JPEG files, up to 5, each up to 7 MB.
`line_items[_].packaging_images[]`         | File    | Photos of the packaging: PNG or JPEG files, up to 5, each up to 7 MB.
`line_items[_].technical_assessment_report`| File    | A technician's assessment of the damage: one PNG, JPEG or PDF file, up to 7 MB.

A line item may appear only once in a request. Files are checked by their name and by their content,
so a file must really be the type its name says.

#### Reasons and the fields each one needs

`id`, `quantity` and `reason` are always required. The other fields depend on the reason:

Reason                       | At fault | Comment  | Product images | Packaging images | Technical report
---------------------------- | -------- | -------- | -------------- | ---------------- | ----------------
`damaged_product`            | required | required | required       | required         | required for products in categories eligible for service
`damaged_packaging`          | required | required | required       | required         | ignored
`partially_damaged_product`  | required | required | required       | required         | ignored
`wrong_product_returned`     | required | required | required       | ignored          | ignored
`return_received_incomplete` | required | required | required       | required         | ignored
`return_not_received`        | required | optional | optional       | optional         | ignored
`delayed_return`             | required | required | ignored        | ignored          | ignored
`item_sent`                  | ignored  | optional | optional       | optional         | ignored
`product_misclassified`      | ignored  | optional | optional       | optional         | ignored

An ignored field is dropped without an error, so it is safe to send every field you have. An optional
comment is kept when sent, and must still be up to 400 characters. A missing required field is refused
with a `422` and the field's error code, for example `at_fault_required`.

Which reasons a line item takes depends on the order:

* A return that is late takes only `delayed_return`.
* A delivery with a missing item takes only `item_sent` or `product_misclassified`.
* Any other return takes `damaged_product`, `damaged_packaging`, `partially_damaged_product`,
  `wrong_product_returned`, `return_received_incomplete` and `return_not_received`.

An order may have several compensation requests. A line item can be claimed again in a later request
until its eligible quantity runs out: what was returned (or not received), minus what earlier requests
already claimed and what was already compensated.

#### Example

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/orders/191128-1234567/compensation_requests \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: multipart/form-data' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -F 'line_items[0][id]=k9Qp2xR4mN' \
  -F 'line_items[0][quantity]=1' \
  -F 'line_items[0][reason]=damaged_packaging' \
  -F 'line_items[0][at_fault]=courier' \
  -F 'line_items[0][comment]=The box arrived crushed on one side' \
  -F 'line_items[0][product_images][]=@/path/to/product.jpg' \
  -F 'line_items[0][packaging_images][]=@/path/to/box.jpg'
</pre>

#### Success Response

The response carries the id of the new compensation request. The order's
[`compensation_requests`](#compensation-requests-on-the-order) list it under the same id.

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "success": true,
  "compensation_request_id": "xK3mPq7Lw2"
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Error Responses

The request is checked in this order: the shop and the order, then each line item. The first problem
found is answered and nothing is filed. A line item's error message starts with the line item's id.

HTTP | Code                                    | When
---- | --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------
400  | `line_items`                            | No line items were sent
404  | `order_error`                           | The order is not one of the shop's orders
409  | `compensation_in_progress`              | Another compensation of the order is being processed at this moment; retry shortly
413  | `file_too_large`                        | A file is over 7 MB
415  | `invalid_file_type`                     | A file is not PNG or JPEG (PDF is also accepted for the technical report), by its name or its content
422  | `not_eligible`                          | The order cannot be compensated; the message gives the reason
422  | `compensation_cap_reached`              | The shop has reached its compensation cap
422  | `pending_physical_review`               | The condition review of the returned products must be submitted first
422  | `line_item_not_found`                   | An `id` that is not one of the order's line items, or a line item sent twice
422  | `line_item_not_eligible`                | Nothing is left to compensate on the line item
422  | `invalid_quantity`                      | The quantity is not a whole number from 1 to the eligible quantity
422  | `invalid_reason`                        | A reason the line item does not take
422  | `at_fault_required`                     | `at_fault` is missing, or not `customer` or `courier`
422  | `comment_required`, `comment_too_long`  | The comment is missing, or over 400 characters
422  | `product_images_required`, `packaging_images_required` | The reason needs photos that were not sent
422  | `technical_assessment_report_required`  | A damaged product in a category eligible for service, without the report
422  | `too_many_images`                       | More than 5 photos of a kind for a line item
422  | `invalid`, `compensation_request_error` | An unexpected error; retry, and contact support if it persists
429  |                                         | Too many requests (see [Rate limit](#rate-limit))

**A line item error** (here, the comment is missing):

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "comment_required",
      "messages": [
        "Line item k9Qp2xR4mN: You must describe the type of the damage for every selected product"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

**Another compensation of the order is in progress:**

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 409 Conflict
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "compensation_in_progress",
      "messages": [
        "Another compensation of this order is in progress. Please try again in a moment."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Rate limit

You can file up to 20 compensation requests a minute with one API token. Past that, requests are
refused with a `429 Too Many Requests` for 30 minutes. One request can carry several line items.

#### Sandbox

Compensation requests for the [demo orders](#test-requests-sandbox) answer `200 { "success": true }`
without filing anything, and without a `compensation_request_id`.


## Error handling

In case of errors, the response will have a representative HTTP status code
in the `4xx` or `5xx` range. The body would contain an array of `errors`.

For example, if you try to accept an order that is already accepted, you will
get a `422` HTTP status code (Unprocessable Entity), with the following body:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
      {
          "code": "order_status",
          "messages": [
              "Order already accepted."
          ]
      }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

And if you try to reject an order that is already rejected, you will
also get a `422` HTTP status code (Unprocessable Entity), with the following body:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
      {
          "code": "order_status",
          "messages": [
              "Order already rejected."
          ]
      }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

## Set as Ready integration flow

This section describes the recommended integration flow for shops enrolled in the
"Set as Ready" program. Read this before testing with the [sandbox](#test-requests-sandbox).

### Updated order response fields

The order response includes two new fields related to Set as Ready:

| Field | Type | Description |
| ----- | ---- | ----------- |
| `set_as_ready_required` | Boolean | `true` if the shop requires explicit set-as-ready (voucher download does not auto-mark as ready). `false` for shops with default behavior. |
| `is_ready_for_dispatch` | Boolean | `true` if the order has been marked as ready for dispatch. `false` otherwise. |

#### Example: enrolled shop, order not yet ready

~~~ json
{
  "order": {
    "code": "260225-123456",
    "state": "accepted",
    "set_as_ready_required": true,
    "is_ready_for_dispatch": false,
    "courier": "Skroutz Last Mile",
    "courier_voucher": "https://b.scdn.gr/path-to/voucher.pdf",
    "pickup_window": {
      "from": "2026-02-27T09:00:00+02:00",
      "to": "2026-02-27T12:00:00+02:00"
    }
  }
}
~~~

#### Example: enrolled shop, order marked as ready

~~~ json
{
  "order": {
    "code": "260225-123456",
    "state": "accepted",
    "set_as_ready_required": true,
    "is_ready_for_dispatch": true,
    "courier": "Skroutz Last Mile",
    "courier_voucher": "https://b.scdn.gr/path-to/voucher.pdf",
    "pickup_window": {
      "from": "2026-02-26T15:00:00+02:00",
      "to": "2026-02-26T18:00:00+02:00"
    }
  }
}
~~~

> In the example above, the `pickup_window` changed because the early set-as-ready
> triggered an auto-reschedule to an earlier date.

### For shops with `set_as_ready_required: true`

<pre>
1. Receive order (webhook or polling)
2. Accept order        → POST /merchants/ecommerce/orders/:code/accept
3. Download voucher    → GET  &lt;courier_voucher URL from order response&gt;
4. Prepare the package
5. Mark as ready       → POST /merchants/ecommerce/orders/:code/set_as_ready
6. Courier picks up
</pre>

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> Steps 3 and 5 are now independent. You can download the voucher at any time,
> but the courier will only schedule a pickup after step 5.

### For shops with `set_as_ready_required: false` (default)

No changes needed. The existing flow continues to work:

<pre>
1. Receive order
2. Accept order        → POST /merchants/ecommerce/orders/:code/accept
3. Download voucher    → GET  &lt;courier_voucher URL from order response&gt;
                         (auto-marks as ready)
4. Courier picks up
</pre>

### Webhook notifications

If you have a [webhook](/smart_cart/orders_webhook) set up, an `order_updated` request is sent
whenever an order is marked as ready for dispatch or that mark is undone, with the
`is_ready_for_dispatch` change in its `changes` object:

~~~ json
{
  "event_type": "order_updated",
  "changes": {
    "is_ready_for_dispatch": {
      "old": false,
      "new": true
    }
  }
}
~~~

See the [webhook documentation](/smart_cart/orders_webhook#order-changes-object) for the full payload.

### Tips for faster pickups

* Mark orders as ready as soon as they are physically prepared — don't wait for the pickup day
* If you mark as ready before 21:00 the day before the scheduled pickup, the system may reschedule to an earlier pickup window
* The earlier you mark as ready, the more likely an earlier pickup slot is available

### FAQ

**Q: What happens if I don't call `set_as_ready`?**
A: The courier will skip your stop. The order will not be picked up.

**Q: Can I still download the voucher without calling `set_as_ready`?**
A: Yes. Voucher download and set-as-ready are independent actions.

**Q: What happens if I call `set_as_not_ready`?**
A: The order is no longer considered ready. The system will attempt to move the pickup back to the
original scheduled date. If that's no longer possible (e.g., the date has passed), the current
pickup schedule remains.

**Q: Does this affect orders shipped with couriers other than Skroutz Last Mile?**
A: No. This only applies to orders shipped via Skroutz Last Mile (SLM).

